    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2623.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 718,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is arguably the most important substance for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The States of Water\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and find in rivers, lakes, and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, or steam rising from a boiling pot.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its electrical polarity (one side is slightly positive, the other slightly negative), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily mix with salt, sugar, and minerals.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, saving aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water is on Earth, about **96.5% of it is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nWater is the essential ingredient for life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made of roughly **60% water**. Our cells, blood, and organs need water to function, transport nutrients, flush out waste, and regulate temperature.\n*   **Ecosystems:** All plants and animals require water to survive. Without it, food chains collapse and ecosystems die.\n*   **Civilization:** Human history has unfolded around water. From ancient rivers like the Nile and the Tigris-Euphrates where the first civilizations were born, to modern agriculture and industry, water drives human society.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that makes existence on Earth possible."
